1
00:00:06,373 --> 00:00:09,142
You know, we’ve had a lot of cars
on this show that are old cars,

2
00:00:09,233 --> 00:00:10,400
they’re classic cars,

3
00:00:10,466 --> 00:00:11,600
they’re beautiful,

4
00:00:11,666 --> 00:00:12,833
they’re well kept up,

5
00:00:12,899 --> 00:00:14,799
they’re little works of art.

6
00:00:15,333 --> 00:00:19,800
And then you have those old cars
that people just used as cars.

7
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:23,266
This is one of those cars.

8
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:29,000
[Jerry] This is a 1958 Porsche
356 Speedster, 

9
00:00:29,066 --> 00:00:31,966
in nonmetallic aquamarine blue
with a black interior.

10
00:00:32,033 --> 00:00:33,500
This is my car.

11
00:00:34,300 --> 00:00:36,466
I use it to go places, to run errands, 

12
00:00:36,533 --> 00:00:40,333
take people around, or drive it just 
for the sheer unbelievable fun of it.

13
00:00:42,533 --> 00:00:47,133
The Speedster is the most iconic, classic,
timeless early Porsche ever made.

14
00:00:47,333 --> 00:00:50,766
It has a tiny race car windshield,
a slippery aerodynamic shape,

15
00:00:51,166 --> 00:00:52,166
cool little bucket seats,

16
00:00:52,233 --> 00:00:54,366
and a powerful 60 horsepower motor.

17
00:00:56,666 --> 00:00:59,266
What makes this 
particular Speedster so special

18
00:00:59,333 --> 00:01:01,699
is that without anyone
taking very good care of it,

19
00:01:01,866 --> 00:01:04,899
it has survived with all its 
original parts intact.

20
00:01:04,966 --> 00:01:06,300
Not every car does.

21
00:01:06,866 --> 00:01:08,968
And for 68 years, it’s been running.

22
00:01:09,035 --> 00:01:10,670
-[starts engine]
-And it’s still running.

23
00:01:10,766 --> 00:01:12,200
And I like things like that.

24
00:01:12,266 --> 00:01:14,800
I like things that weren’t 
that well taken care of,

25
00:01:14,866 --> 00:01:17,200
but just hung in there
and survived anyway.

26
00:01:17,277 --> 00:01:19,279
[distorted voice over PA]

27
00:01:22,133 --> 00:01:24,700
It really reminds me of 
my very special guest today...

28
00:01:24,751 --> 00:01:26,553
Yeah, the cops are coming, Jerry.

29
00:01:26,653 --> 00:01:27,954
[Jerry] Mr. Norm MacDonald.

30
00:01:28,033 --> 00:01:31,300
I don’t want to make a friend,
I just want to find out about this steak.

31
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:33,933
Norm MacDonald is one of my 
favorite comedians.

32
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:35,800
He is the former host of Weekend Update,

33
00:01:36,066 --> 00:01:37,600
and is now the host of...

34
00:01:37,666 --> 00:01:39,966
Good evening, everybody.
Welcome to Norm MacDonald Live.

35
00:01:40,399 --> 00:01:41,433
[phone ringing]

36
00:01:41,899 --> 00:01:42,899
[Norm] Hello?

37
00:01:42,966 --> 00:01:44,199
[Jerry] Hey Norm, it’s Jerry.

38
00:01:44,266 --> 00:01:46,199
[Norm] Hi, Jerry. Did you bring your car?

39
00:01:46,266 --> 00:01:49,633
[Jerry] I did. Yeah, it’s such a beautiful
day, you’re not going to believe it.

40
00:01:49,699 --> 00:01:51,833
-[Norm] I’m happy.
-[Jerry] How do you feel?

41
00:01:51,899 --> 00:01:53,566
[Norm] This small talk’s helping.

42
00:01:53,633 --> 00:01:57,466
[Jerry] To me, a cup of coffee with
a comedian is my idea of a perfect day.

43
00:01:57,533 --> 00:01:59,366
[Norm] Then this is 
the perfect show for you.

44
00:01:59,433 --> 00:02:00,433
[Jerry] Yeah.

45
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:02,233
[Norm]
Would be like if I had a show called

46
00:02:02,300 --> 00:02:04,833
Eating Count Chocula All By Myself.

47
00:02:05,133 --> 00:02:06,233
[Jerry laughs]

48
00:02:06,693 --> 00:02:08,628
[upbeat music playing]

49
00:02:09,799 --> 00:02:14,799
I’m Jerry Seinfeld, and this is 
Comedians in Car Getting Coffee.

50
00:02:17,866 --> 00:02:19,166
-Hey, Norm.
-Hi, Jerry.

51
00:02:19,233 --> 00:02:20,700
Good Christ.

52
00:02:21,533 --> 00:02:24,700
God damn, man. Holy crow.

53
00:02:24,766 --> 00:02:25,900
Oh, God.

54
00:02:26,400 --> 00:02:28,733
-What the [beep]?
-Hey! [cackles]

55
00:02:29,066 --> 00:02:30,166
This is a car?

56
00:02:34,933 --> 00:02:37,233
[Norm] I was hoping you’d bring
a tiny, ancient car.

57
00:02:37,299 --> 00:02:38,766
[Jerry] I didn’t figure on rain.

58
00:02:38,833 --> 00:02:40,066
[Norm] Oh, no.

59
00:02:40,133 --> 00:02:42,566
[Jerry] I’m sorry about that.
A rusty car in the rain.

60
00:02:42,633 --> 00:02:44,133
[Norm] A rusty car in the rain.

61
00:02:44,200 --> 00:02:46,133
[Jerry] That’s the Norm MacDonald story,
isn’t it?

62
00:02:46,200 --> 00:02:47,833
-[Norm] A rusty car in the rain?
-[Jerry] Yeah.

63
00:02:47,900 --> 00:02:49,400
[Norm] Sounds like
one of those sex things.

64
00:02:49,466 --> 00:02:51,700
How much for a rusty car in the rain?

65
00:02:52,966 --> 00:02:55,900
This car is working amazingly well
for the conditions that we’re in,

66
00:02:55,966 --> 00:02:57,500
which really couldn’t be worse.

67
00:02:58,500 --> 00:03:00,600
[Norm] Now you’re changing lanes,
I notice.

68
00:03:00,766 --> 00:03:04,233
Let’s make a list
of incredibly banal observations.

69
00:03:06,366 --> 00:03:09,600
My mother will be in a car
and we’ll be driving, she’ll go,

70
00:03:09,666 --> 00:03:14,433
"Ralphs!" She’ll just
read words on buildings.

71
00:03:14,500 --> 00:03:16,566
[Jerry] Here’s another thing I find
that old people do.

72
00:03:16,633 --> 00:03:20,600
They’re completely surprised when waiters
want to know what you want to eat.

73
00:03:20,666 --> 00:03:23,466
They’re just like, "Oh."

74
00:03:23,533 --> 00:03:24,733
"Oh," and then...

75
00:03:24,799 --> 00:03:26,133
"Oh, okay."

76
00:03:26,966 --> 00:03:27,866
Caught unaware.

77
00:03:27,933 --> 00:03:30,299
"What would you like?"
Always seem to be caught off guard.

78
00:03:33,079 --> 00:03:36,349
One time I went on,
it was like a, a dance floor.

79
00:03:36,433 --> 00:03:38,933
And they gave me a Mr. Mic.
Do you know what a Mr. Mic is?

80
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:41,066
-Of course.
-So, I’m holding the speaker

81
00:03:41,133 --> 00:03:43,899
and the mic and talking,

82
00:03:43,966 --> 00:03:46,533
and then a guy comes in
from the street, you know...

83
00:03:46,600 --> 00:03:47,700
-Yeah.
-And there’s no sign

84
00:03:47,766 --> 00:03:49,433
advertising for me, right?

85
00:03:49,500 --> 00:03:52,833
So, he thinks I’m a psychotic,
you know, person.

86
00:03:52,899 --> 00:03:54,833
And so he starts approaching me, you know.

87
00:03:54,899 --> 00:03:57,399
Like, you would say,
"Give me the gun," you know.

88
00:03:57,466 --> 00:04:00,366
And I’m like, "No, no,
I’m the performer. I’m the entertainer."

89
00:04:02,666 --> 00:04:05,500
I did a similar gig at a disco in Queens.

90
00:04:05,566 --> 00:04:06,666
And what happened in Queens?

91
00:04:06,733 --> 00:04:08,233
It died a miserable death.

92
00:04:08,299 --> 00:04:11,799
I went to get paid and the owner went,
"I didn’t even know you were on."

93
00:04:12,600 --> 00:04:13,899
I said, "Nobody did."

94
00:04:16,033 --> 00:04:17,766
Are you allowed to swear on this program?

95
00:04:17,833 --> 00:04:19,666
Sure. But I will beep it.

96
00:04:19,726 --> 00:04:22,195
-You know how I am with the words.
-Not gonna use those words.

97
00:04:22,266 --> 00:04:27,033
Well, listen. As I get older,
I understand the value of clean comedy.

98
00:04:27,099 --> 00:04:27,933
Oh, yeah?

99
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:31,099
And how it’s a superior, now you’re
going to say it’s not superior,

100
00:04:31,166 --> 00:04:33,166
but it is superior.

101
00:04:33,233 --> 00:04:34,466
It’s totally superior.

102
00:04:34,533 --> 00:04:36,566
-You do think it is? Okay.
-Completely.

103
00:04:38,599 --> 00:04:40,866
Would you ever consider
changing your style?

104
00:04:40,933 --> 00:04:42,266
Yes, yes, yes.

105
00:04:42,333 --> 00:04:45,733
I considered for a while because,

106
00:04:45,800 --> 00:04:49,533
you know, I loved Rodney
when he would go on The Tonight Show,

107
00:04:49,599 --> 00:04:51,500
-and just destroy...
-Yeah.

108
00:04:51,566 --> 00:04:52,966
...for eight minutes. Then on the couch,

109
00:04:53,033 --> 00:04:54,633
-destroy for another five minutes.
-Yeah.

110
00:04:54,700 --> 00:04:56,599
And then at the end,
"How’s your week, Johnny?"

111
00:04:56,666 --> 00:04:58,766
-You know, after like just...
-Yeah.

112
00:04:58,833 --> 00:05:01,000
...non-stop knockout punches.

113
00:05:01,100 --> 00:05:02,535
What do you want to do now, huh?

114
00:05:02,700 --> 00:05:03,933
[audience laughing]

115
00:05:04,266 --> 00:05:05,566
That wraps it up, huh?

116
00:05:05,633 --> 00:05:07,366
[Jerry] I’ll go as far as to say this.

117
00:05:08,099 --> 00:05:10,733
I think that cursing ruined his act.

118
00:05:10,866 --> 00:05:12,000
No, I would agree with you.

119
00:05:12,066 --> 00:05:15,099
I had no idea,
as a matter of fact, I was so naive,

120
00:05:15,166 --> 00:05:16,966
I only knew him from
Tonight Show appearances.

121
00:05:17,033 --> 00:05:18,000
Yeah.

122
00:05:18,051 --> 00:05:21,120
And it wasn’t until I got to Los Angeles,
and I was like, "Whoa!"

123
00:05:21,200 --> 00:05:22,933
I think it undermined his charm.

124
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:24,466
Yeah, even the way he, you know,

125
00:05:24,533 --> 00:05:27,133
he used to dress so beautifully
with the suits.

126
00:05:27,200 --> 00:05:28,633
-Yeah.
-And the character.

127
00:05:28,700 --> 00:05:31,200
And then I go-- Then I went,
what’s the character now?

128
00:05:31,266 --> 00:05:35,033
A guy with bleached blonde hair and
a Hawaiian shirt, you know what I mean?

129
00:05:35,099 --> 00:05:36,733
-It didn’t seem to make any sense.
-Yeah.

130
00:05:38,033 --> 00:05:39,433
[Norm] I was just in, uh,

131
00:05:39,500 --> 00:05:42,166
-Las Vegas, Nevada.
-Yeah.

132
00:05:42,233 --> 00:05:43,666
And I was in the elevator.

133
00:05:43,733 --> 00:05:48,300
So it was just a man and his wife,
and the man says to his wife,

134
00:05:48,366 --> 00:05:50,766
"I don’t care what I told you,
give me the money."

135
00:05:51,366 --> 00:05:53,599
Or the husband yelling at the wife,

136
00:05:53,666 --> 00:05:57,133
"How do you lose $150
in a slot machine?"

137
00:05:57,200 --> 00:05:59,533
And she says, "You lost 15,000."

138
00:05:59,599 --> 00:06:01,366
He says, "Yeah, but I know how to gamble."

139
00:06:01,466 --> 00:06:02,633
[laughs]

140
00:06:03,766 --> 00:06:07,900
The guy who loses, you know, he says,
"My God, I had a terrible day today.

141
00:06:07,966 --> 00:06:11,000
Lost 15 out of 15 in college football,

142
00:06:11,066 --> 00:06:16,633
I lost eight out of eight in baseball,
you know.

143
00:06:16,700 --> 00:06:21,233
I lost six out of six in soccer.

144
00:06:21,299 --> 00:06:23,599
You know, I don’t know
what I’m going to do. You know."

145
00:06:23,666 --> 00:06:27,266
And uh, the other guy goes,
"Well, there’s a hockey game on tonight."

146
00:06:27,320 --> 00:06:29,222
He goes, "I don’t know
anything about hockey."

147
00:06:32,866 --> 00:06:34,099
You like sweaters?

148
00:06:34,833 --> 00:06:37,366
Sweaters? Are you kidding me?

149
00:06:42,566 --> 00:06:44,099
How are you doing financially?

150
00:06:44,166 --> 00:06:46,266
Well, how much money does a fella need?

151
00:06:46,739 --> 00:06:48,608
You know what I mean? You need...

152
00:06:48,674 --> 00:06:51,110
Of course I know what you mean.
It’s a very simple question.

153
00:06:51,200 --> 00:06:53,133
That’s right. Why do I keep saying it?

154
00:06:54,966 --> 00:06:59,500
Now do you think Cosby’s legacy
will be hurt?

155
00:06:59,566 --> 00:07:01,000
-Yeah.
-You do, huh?

156
00:07:01,066 --> 00:07:03,500
I mean, there’s a comedian,
Patton Oswalt, he told me,

157
00:07:03,566 --> 00:07:07,566
"I think the worst part of
the Cosby thing was the hypocrisy."

158
00:07:08,566 --> 00:07:09,799
And I disagreed.

159
00:07:10,099 --> 00:07:13,266
-You disagreed with that?
-Yeah, I thought it was the raping.

160
00:07:15,566 --> 00:07:18,333
Now, it’s my feeling that
most rapists are hypocrites.

161
00:07:18,371 --> 00:07:19,705
You don’t meet many who go,

162
00:07:19,772 --> 00:07:24,443
"I like raping and I know
it’s not politically correct, but by God."

163
00:07:24,533 --> 00:07:27,666
People go, "Well, he’s not being
a hypocrite and that’s the worst part."

164
00:07:29,633 --> 00:07:30,866
Where are we, Jerry?

165
00:07:30,917 --> 00:07:32,919
Man, I could use some hot coffee

166
00:07:32,985 --> 00:07:34,187
-and soup.
-God, I could, too.

167
00:07:34,266 --> 00:07:37,366
I don’t know how my honeycomb
sweater... Look at that sweater, huh?

168
00:07:37,423 --> 00:07:39,325
It reminds me of the Bernie Madoff outfit,

169
00:07:39,400 --> 00:07:41,900
when they accosted him
outside the building.

170
00:07:41,966 --> 00:07:43,566
Think this is similar.

171
00:07:43,633 --> 00:07:46,266
You’re saying that’s a good thing.
Bernie Madoff had a lot of money.

172
00:07:47,166 --> 00:07:48,433
[Norm] Good Christ.

173
00:07:48,734 --> 00:07:50,736
[groaning]

174
00:07:51,604 --> 00:07:55,107
Well, here we are. Isn’t this wonderful?
Look at this, huh?

175
00:07:55,200 --> 00:07:57,133
Everybody slams the door on this car.

176
00:07:57,200 --> 00:07:58,400
You don’t need to slam it.

177
00:07:58,466 --> 00:08:00,966
Here we go, me and Norm MacDonald

178
00:08:01,033 --> 00:08:03,266
-on the worst day of the year.
-Yeah.

179
00:08:12,592 --> 00:08:14,393
-[Norm sighs]
-I’m happy now.

180
00:08:14,466 --> 00:08:15,466
-You are?
-Yeah.

181
00:08:15,533 --> 00:08:17,099
Because you’re inside?

182
00:08:17,166 --> 00:08:18,466
-And I’m with you.
-Yeah, yeah.

183
00:08:18,533 --> 00:08:19,733
And I’m in a diner.

184
00:08:19,799 --> 00:08:21,799
-Aren’t diners beautiful?
-I love them.

185
00:08:21,901 --> 00:08:23,736
-[Jerry] How are you?
-Good afternoon.

186
00:08:23,803 --> 00:08:25,872
-What is your accent?
-[server] Ireland.

187
00:08:25,938 --> 00:08:28,875
-Yeah? Beautiful accent.
-Ireland? Yeah, it’s beautiful.

188
00:08:28,941 --> 00:08:31,310
-Can I get you something to drink?
-Hot coffee please, yeah.

189
00:08:31,400 --> 00:08:33,299
-Yes, I also. Yes, yes.
-[server] Okay.

190
00:08:40,052 --> 00:08:41,387
-Here you are.
-Thank you.

191
00:08:41,466 --> 00:08:42,500
[Norm] Look at that.

192
00:08:43,466 --> 00:08:45,100
These are great cups, by the way.

193
00:08:46,259 --> 00:08:49,128
-This is working out great.
-It is. [chuckles]

194
00:08:49,233 --> 00:08:51,866
-This cup is like perfect for coffee.
-Yeah.

195
00:08:55,268 --> 00:08:58,604
-Man, that’s coffee. It’s good coffee.
-Yeah, that’s really great coffee.

196
00:08:58,671 --> 00:09:01,173
-Great.
-And especially after the wet cold

197
00:09:01,240 --> 00:09:02,775
-that we just came from.
-Whoo.

198
00:09:03,133 --> 00:09:04,566
-And now we’re warm.
-Isn’t this fun?

199
00:09:04,633 --> 00:09:06,500
-Now we’re warm.
-Yeah, well, we’re warm.

200
00:09:06,566 --> 00:09:10,400
Not only by the coffee, but by fellowship.

201
00:09:10,466 --> 00:09:12,199
And the bonhomie.

202
00:09:13,933 --> 00:09:15,100
[snickers]

203
00:09:22,066 --> 00:09:23,666
I almost got you there.

204
00:09:24,230 --> 00:09:27,800
I almost got you to spit up
on "bonhomie."

205
00:09:28,699 --> 00:09:31,100
Is that the goal of comedy?

206
00:09:31,166 --> 00:09:32,666
-Yes.
-Like, I guess, huh?

207
00:09:32,733 --> 00:09:35,533
It’s a good feeling. To make
another comedian spit up a little bit.

208
00:09:35,600 --> 00:09:36,666
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

209
00:09:37,866 --> 00:09:39,166
Do you not like the saucer?

210
00:09:39,211 --> 00:09:40,980
-Yeah, no, no.
-Oh.

211
00:09:42,533 --> 00:09:43,833
Man alive.

212
00:09:45,433 --> 00:09:47,366
So what I was going to ask you to do,

213
00:09:47,433 --> 00:09:49,433
the Nixon Watergate.

214
00:09:49,500 --> 00:09:51,566
I have to hear that again.

215
00:09:51,633 --> 00:09:54,400
I would do it because
I love doing Nixon so much.

216
00:09:54,466 --> 00:09:56,666
David Frost is interviewing Richard Nixon.

217
00:09:56,733 --> 00:09:57,566
[Jerry] Right.

218
00:09:57,766 --> 00:10:00,833
Because Watergate was so ridiculous,

219
00:10:00,900 --> 00:10:03,733
Nixon would use that for his own benefit.

220
00:10:03,800 --> 00:10:06,699
So, at one point, David Frost goes,

221
00:10:06,766 --> 00:10:10,766
"Now Mr. Nixon, when E. Howard Hunt
and Gordon Liddy

222
00:10:10,866 --> 00:10:12,966
entered the Watergate Hotel

223
00:10:13,033 --> 00:10:16,533
and broke into the room,
at that moment,

224
00:10:16,582 --> 00:10:21,554
they were carrying listening devices
and, and wigs."

225
00:10:21,633 --> 00:10:23,833
Richard Nixon goes, "Well,

226
00:10:23,900 --> 00:10:26,800
I, uh, I suppose that

227
00:10:26,866 --> 00:10:30,400
they were carrying listening devices,

228
00:10:30,466 --> 00:10:34,633
perhaps you could call them
hearing devices. And wigs."

229
00:10:39,566 --> 00:10:42,600
You like the old shows
that were very high concept?

230
00:10:42,666 --> 00:10:45,400
Did you care for any of those sitcoms?

231
00:10:45,466 --> 00:10:48,500
I liked I Dream of Jeannie
because I love the space program.

232
00:10:48,566 --> 00:10:50,766
-It was Major Nelson and Dr. Bellows.
-I love those...

233
00:10:50,833 --> 00:10:53,000
It was Dr. Bellows
'cause he was the psychiatrist.

234
00:10:53,066 --> 00:10:54,933
Gonna have astronauts,
gotta have a psychiatrist.

235
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:56,533
-Why?
-Yeah, yeah, it's true. Yeah.

236
00:10:56,600 --> 00:10:58,199
And he could shake it off, you know.

237
00:10:58,266 --> 00:11:01,133
Somebody would appear, disappear.

238
00:11:01,199 --> 00:11:02,500
It would roll right off.

239
00:11:02,566 --> 00:11:03,866
Yeah.

240
00:11:06,400 --> 00:11:07,966
-You remember Kojak?
-Telly Savalas.

241
00:11:08,033 --> 00:11:10,100
-Telly Savalas.
-With the lollipop.

242
00:11:10,166 --> 00:11:11,800
Yeah, he had the lollipop going.

243
00:11:11,866 --> 00:11:15,666
So, for the pilot,
it was Crocker’s first day.

244
00:11:15,733 --> 00:11:17,266
You know, Kevin Dobson played Crocker.

245
00:11:17,333 --> 00:11:18,333
Sure. Crocker.

246
00:11:18,400 --> 00:11:19,900
So he was just out of police academy.

247
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:21,300
-Green.
-Yeah.

248
00:11:21,366 --> 00:11:22,800
They kept saying how green he was.

249
00:11:22,866 --> 00:11:25,166
Kojak had been around a long time,

250
00:11:25,233 --> 00:11:26,433
he knew everything.

251
00:11:26,485 --> 00:11:28,721
So he’s, you know, has this green kid

252
00:11:28,788 --> 00:11:31,290
and he doesn’t know if he even wants
to be working with him.

253
00:11:31,366 --> 00:11:33,466
So, what was happening was,

254
00:11:33,533 --> 00:11:35,333
prostitutes were being murdered,

255
00:11:35,400 --> 00:11:36,433
-you know?
-Oh, my.

256
00:11:36,500 --> 00:11:38,100
He goes, "Go down

257
00:11:38,166 --> 00:11:41,533
and try to get some information
from the prostitute’s mother."

258
00:11:41,966 --> 00:11:44,900
So, a little while later,
Crocker comes back.

259
00:11:45,066 --> 00:11:46,766
"I talked to her mother.

260
00:11:46,833 --> 00:11:50,300
She wasn’t a prostitute at all.
She was a good, good girl."

261
00:11:50,366 --> 00:11:52,699
You know? So, Kojak's seen it all, right?

262
00:11:53,033 --> 00:11:55,400
So, he takes his lollipop, he goes,

263
00:11:55,466 --> 00:11:58,266
"Yep. She was a good girl.

264
00:11:58,333 --> 00:12:00,333
Mama’s apple pie.

265
00:12:00,400 --> 00:12:02,633
The Fourth of July. She was a hooker!"

266
00:12:03,400 --> 00:12:04,933
[laughing]

267
00:12:07,733 --> 00:12:11,100
Crocker was too green to see it, you know?

268
00:12:12,400 --> 00:12:14,900
So that’s a funny one
that anyone can do at home.

269
00:12:17,566 --> 00:12:19,633
This hot sauce is so hot.

270
00:12:19,699 --> 00:12:21,400
Do you like really hot things?

271
00:12:21,466 --> 00:12:23,800
I do, but that is unbelievably hot.

272
00:12:23,866 --> 00:12:25,300
Okay, I'm gonna try this.

273
00:12:25,344 --> 00:12:27,379
-Do you like hot sauce?
-Oh, I hate it.

274
00:12:28,199 --> 00:12:30,266
But you know, you gotta try
new things in life.

275
00:12:30,333 --> 00:12:32,033
-No you don’t.
-Yeah, you’re right.

276
00:12:33,100 --> 00:12:35,333
I don’t know if you had this
when you were young,

277
00:12:35,400 --> 00:12:37,066
but we’d always go, you know,

278
00:12:37,133 --> 00:12:40,333
"We’ll do heroin... when we’re 80!"

279
00:12:40,400 --> 00:12:43,266
-The idea being that once you hit 80...
-Yeah.

280
00:12:43,329 --> 00:12:44,997
-You know, what do you got to lose?
-Right.

281
00:12:45,064 --> 00:12:48,267
Might as well do heroin.
But now that I grow older, I realize,

282
00:12:48,333 --> 00:12:52,500
it’s not like you become fearless
when you become older. It’s the opposite.

283
00:12:52,566 --> 00:12:56,400
You know, I remember my grandmother,
I walked into the room once

284
00:12:56,466 --> 00:13:00,466
and she had a bruise
that went from her wrist

285
00:13:00,533 --> 00:13:02,333
all the way up to her shoulder.

286
00:13:02,433 --> 00:13:05,966
-Big, purple, yellow-ish bruise.
-Wow!

287
00:13:06,033 --> 00:13:09,266
And I said, "My God, Grandma.
Where did you get that?" "The wind."

288
00:13:13,466 --> 00:13:14,800
So, she’s not doing heroin.

289
00:13:16,900 --> 00:13:19,733
When I first started
I used to do a joke about,

290
00:13:19,800 --> 00:13:22,266
when I was a kid my parents
were always telling me

291
00:13:22,333 --> 00:13:24,400
how milk was a nickel,
a car was a quarter,

292
00:13:24,466 --> 00:13:27,766
and I thought, how could the world
change that much again?

293
00:13:27,833 --> 00:13:30,000
Will I be saying to my kids,

294
00:13:30,066 --> 00:13:32,533
you know, when I was a boy,
no dogs could vote.

295
00:13:33,566 --> 00:13:35,300
They were just pets.

296
00:13:35,933 --> 00:13:37,033
I remember that joke.

297
00:13:37,166 --> 00:13:38,566
-You do?
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

298
00:13:38,633 --> 00:13:41,366
-No dogs could vote. That's awesome.
-Wow.

299
00:13:43,666 --> 00:13:45,366
You don't have a child.

300
00:13:45,433 --> 00:13:47,433
-Pardon me?
-You don't have a child.

301
00:13:47,500 --> 00:13:49,800
-So, you don't have to worry about...
-I don't?

302
00:13:49,866 --> 00:13:50,866
I have three.

303
00:13:52,033 --> 00:13:53,066
You have three daughters?

304
00:13:53,133 --> 00:13:54,800
I have a daughter and two sons.

305
00:13:54,866 --> 00:13:56,033
What?

306
00:13:58,466 --> 00:14:02,566
I guess we can agree to disagree,
but I thought you had no children.

307
00:14:05,211 --> 00:14:07,546
-Well, that's what you thought, then.
-Whoa!

308
00:14:09,633 --> 00:14:12,400
Son of a gun, that’s hotter
than a two-dollar pistol.

309
00:14:13,633 --> 00:14:15,466
Man alive.

310
00:14:17,400 --> 00:14:22,033
How do you feel about John Hinckley
walking the streets of DC?

311
00:14:22,094 --> 00:14:23,896
And yet attempted murder and murder

312
00:14:23,963 --> 00:14:25,531
-are the same thing. Yeah.
-Same thing.

313
00:14:25,599 --> 00:14:27,133
Well, it’s a different skill level.

314
00:14:29,266 --> 00:14:31,733
What about manslaughter?

315
00:14:31,800 --> 00:14:34,133
Less than murder, but sounds way worse.

316
00:14:35,966 --> 00:14:38,966
The other one is if you kill
more than one person,

317
00:14:39,033 --> 00:14:41,633
that’s called "special circumstances."

318
00:14:41,699 --> 00:14:44,199
Which sounds to me
like a custom-made jacket.

319
00:14:45,433 --> 00:14:46,866
Now, you can buy one off the rack,

320
00:14:46,933 --> 00:14:49,833
but we also have these
special circumstances.

321
00:14:49,900 --> 00:14:51,933
-A bespoke jacket.
-Yeah.

322
00:14:54,266 --> 00:14:57,666
It just sounds like a very elegant term
for murdering lots of people.

323
00:14:57,733 --> 00:15:02,066
Well, let’s just call it...
special circumstances.

324
00:15:02,133 --> 00:15:05,966
One of the most hellish things
was that Richard Speck, you know...

325
00:15:06,033 --> 00:15:07,533
-Yeah.
-...busted in and killed,

326
00:15:07,599 --> 00:15:09,500
and raped-- killed all the nurses.

327
00:15:09,566 --> 00:15:11,000
But I remember he was charged with,

328
00:15:11,066 --> 00:15:14,099
whatever, nine counts of rape,
nine counts of murder,

329
00:15:14,166 --> 00:15:16,133
and one count of breaking and entering.

330
00:15:18,199 --> 00:15:20,966
Not only did you enter,
you broke something.

331
00:15:21,866 --> 00:15:23,866
-Yeah, I never thought of that.
-Yeah.

332
00:15:23,933 --> 00:15:25,866
That doorknob is broken.

333
00:15:26,266 --> 00:15:28,000
-Who’s going to pay for that?
-You’re right.

334
00:15:30,199 --> 00:15:33,566
When I first came to Hollywood,
I wrote for Roseanne, you know.

335
00:15:33,633 --> 00:15:36,599
And I remember looking
at the first script, you know,

336
00:15:36,666 --> 00:15:39,766
because when you come in
from a standup perspective,

337
00:15:39,833 --> 00:15:41,466
and you read these jokes,

338
00:15:41,533 --> 00:15:43,133
you go, "Every joke’s gonna bomb."

339
00:15:43,199 --> 00:15:45,633
Like, you know,
and I remember telling people, I go,

340
00:15:45,699 --> 00:15:49,033
"This whole show, it’s just-- people
are going to be booing." You know?

341
00:15:49,081 --> 00:15:51,183
-And everybody was, "No, no, it’s fine."
-Yeah.

342
00:15:51,266 --> 00:15:53,000
Because I didn’t know how it worked.

343
00:15:53,066 --> 00:15:53,900
-Yeah.
-I didn’t know

344
00:15:53,966 --> 00:15:56,300
that the audience is complicit
in the whole affair.

345
00:15:56,366 --> 00:15:57,566
That’s funny.

346
00:15:57,633 --> 00:16:01,800
So, you know, if they don’t laugh,
the warm-up guy

347
00:16:01,866 --> 00:16:03,500
-stops the show...
-Yeah.

348
00:16:03,562 --> 00:16:07,333
...berates them, and says
"Whenever the guy stops talking, laugh."

349
00:16:07,400 --> 00:16:08,366
-You know?
-Right.

350
00:16:08,433 --> 00:16:11,599
But how insulting is it
when you’re watching TV

351
00:16:11,666 --> 00:16:14,400
and every joke kills?

352
00:16:14,466 --> 00:16:18,699
I’m comforted by the fact that
most of these shows tank.

353
00:16:18,766 --> 00:16:22,233
Here’s a company, okay,
and a television network.

354
00:16:22,300 --> 00:16:24,033
"We’re going to make
a piece of entertainment.

355
00:16:24,099 --> 00:16:28,333
We’re going to bring it to your
living room, give it to you for free."

356
00:16:28,533 --> 00:16:31,199
And the public says, "We don’t want it."

357
00:16:33,333 --> 00:16:35,699
How bad is that show?

358
00:16:39,033 --> 00:16:40,933
If you don’t like a guy’s act,

359
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:43,066
any chance you’d like him?

360
00:16:47,066 --> 00:16:47,900
Hmm.

361
00:16:51,300 --> 00:16:52,433
-No.
-No?

362
00:16:55,066 --> 00:16:57,066
When I grew up with cartoons,

363
00:16:57,133 --> 00:16:59,366
there were good guys and bad guys.

364
00:16:59,433 --> 00:17:02,766
But do you like this move
to conflicted characters?

365
00:17:02,833 --> 00:17:04,133
[Jerry] What do you think?

366
00:17:04,200 --> 00:17:05,966
-I don’t think you do.
-[Jerry] No.

367
00:17:06,033 --> 00:17:08,666
-I think you like good versus bad.
-Yes.

368
00:17:08,733 --> 00:17:13,066
This is my big beef with,
for example, James Bond.

369
00:17:13,133 --> 00:17:14,966
You have a license to kill,

370
00:17:15,033 --> 00:17:16,766
you have the fastest car in the world,

371
00:17:16,833 --> 00:17:19,033
every girl wants to go to bed with you.

372
00:17:19,099 --> 00:17:20,099
How about a smile?

373
00:17:22,833 --> 00:17:25,399
-Yeah, right.
-How about a little smile once in a while?

374
00:17:25,466 --> 00:17:28,800
-What’s all the internal strife?
-Yeah. Right, right.

375
00:17:30,449 --> 00:17:34,153
Your last Letterman with
the World War I and II bit.

376
00:17:34,633 --> 00:17:39,133
The only country that really worries me
is the country of Germany.

377
00:17:40,025 --> 00:17:42,261
I don’t know if you guys are
history buffs or not, but...

378
00:17:42,333 --> 00:17:43,466
[audience laughing]

379
00:17:44,433 --> 00:17:45,666
[clapping]

380
00:17:46,033 --> 00:17:49,066
In the early part
of the previous century,

381
00:17:49,133 --> 00:17:51,300
Germany decided to go to war.

382
00:17:51,366 --> 00:17:53,866
And who did they go to war with?

383
00:17:53,933 --> 00:17:55,133
The world.

384
00:17:55,200 --> 00:17:56,233
[audience laughing]

385
00:17:57,433 --> 00:18:01,300
And so you figure that would take
about five seconds for the world to win.

386
00:18:01,366 --> 00:18:04,200
But, no, it was actually close.

387
00:18:04,333 --> 00:18:05,700
[audience laughing]

388
00:18:06,566 --> 00:18:12,000
Then about 30 years pass,
and Germany decides, again,

389
00:18:12,066 --> 00:18:16,233
to go to war, and again,
it chooses as its enemy, the world.

390
00:18:16,300 --> 00:18:17,633
[audience laughing]

391
00:18:20,333 --> 00:18:22,633
And this time they have that guy...
[mimicking Hitler]

392
00:18:22,700 --> 00:18:24,000
-That guy.
-[audience laughing]

393
00:18:24,200 --> 00:18:28,166
I have never heard more comics
talk about a bit.

394
00:18:28,233 --> 00:18:31,233
And I heard
that you put a lot of work into that.

395
00:18:31,300 --> 00:18:32,500
God damn right I did.

396
00:18:32,566 --> 00:18:34,866
But you’d think at that point
the world would go,

397
00:18:34,933 --> 00:18:37,433
"Listen, Germany, here’s the deal.

398
00:18:37,500 --> 00:18:39,633
You don’t get to be a country no more

399
00:18:40,233 --> 00:18:43,800
on account of you keep
attacking the world."

400
00:18:43,866 --> 00:18:44,866
[audience laughing]

401
00:18:46,700 --> 00:18:49,266
[Jerry] Think firemen should have
the day off when it’s raining?

402
00:18:49,333 --> 00:18:50,466
[laughing]

403
00:18:50,696 --> 00:18:52,331
[Jerry] Give them the day off.

404
00:18:59,333 --> 00:19:00,633
Holy crow.

405
00:19:00,700 --> 00:19:03,566
You say more things
that nobody says anymore.

406
00:19:03,633 --> 00:19:04,666
I...

407
00:19:05,600 --> 00:19:09,033
"Holy crow."
I haven’t heard that since ’58.

408
00:19:11,433 --> 00:19:12,500
[groaning]

409
00:19:13,066 --> 00:19:13,899
Jesus.

410
00:19:17,600 --> 00:19:19,899
My son, when I went to see him in college,

411
00:19:19,966 --> 00:19:22,600
you don’t know if your child’s gay,
you know what I mean?

412
00:19:22,666 --> 00:19:24,100
-Right.
-Until he tells you.

413
00:19:24,166 --> 00:19:27,233
So I’m always like,
super pro-gay just in case.

414
00:19:27,300 --> 00:19:29,333
-Right.
-So I’m like...

415
00:19:29,399 --> 00:19:33,133
I’m like, "My friend just came out.
We’re so happy, you know.

416
00:19:33,200 --> 00:19:35,399
It’s good that he came out. He’s gay."

417
00:19:35,466 --> 00:19:39,800
And then I began to think,
maybe my son thinks I’m gay now.

418
00:19:41,066 --> 00:19:43,566
What if we each think each other is gay?

419
00:19:45,533 --> 00:19:48,966
Leading secretive heterosexual lives.

420
00:19:49,033 --> 00:19:52,633
Acting gay around each other
to make the other one not feel bad.

421
00:19:52,700 --> 00:19:53,533
Right.

422
00:19:55,500 --> 00:19:58,566
Larry Miller has the greatest joke
I’ve ever heard about Canada.

423
00:19:58,633 --> 00:20:00,200
He says, "You go to Canada,

424
00:20:00,266 --> 00:20:05,533
it’s like as if aliens had captured you,
brought you back to their planet,

425
00:20:05,600 --> 00:20:08,266
and rebuilt what they thought was Earth,

426
00:20:08,333 --> 00:20:10,233
but they can only see it
through telescopes.

427
00:20:10,766 --> 00:20:12,933
And you would get out,
they would release you,

428
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:17,000
and you would walk into this,
what seemed like America,

429
00:20:17,066 --> 00:20:18,933
and you’d go, 'Oh, great, I’m home.'

430
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:23,466
And then, you would slowly realize
that something is terribly wrong.

431
00:20:25,733 --> 00:20:28,233
You’d go, 'Blue Dot Gasoline?

432
00:20:28,300 --> 00:20:29,633
The Ford Commander?

433
00:20:29,700 --> 00:20:31,533
What are these things?

434
00:20:31,600 --> 00:20:34,066
This is not my home. I’m not home.'"

435
00:20:34,133 --> 00:20:37,566
I’m impressed that you appreciate
the humor of that, as a Canadian.

436
00:20:40,066 --> 00:20:44,633
You really have a lot to accomplish
in your remaining years?

437
00:20:44,700 --> 00:20:50,766
My entire adult life is just
making comedy stuff.

438
00:20:50,833 --> 00:20:53,800
And for some reason, it’s important to me
to make as much of it as I can.

439
00:20:53,866 --> 00:20:54,700
Yeah.

440
00:20:54,766 --> 00:20:56,000
-I imagine...
-Do you...

441
00:20:56,066 --> 00:20:58,233
...a woodchuck has a similar mindset.

442
00:20:58,300 --> 00:20:59,300
-A woodchuck?
-Yeah.

443
00:20:59,366 --> 00:21:03,800
A woodchuck thinks, "I’m a woodchuck.
I’ve got to chuck wood...

444
00:21:04,800 --> 00:21:08,033
as much as I can 'cause that’s my thing."

445
00:21:13,633 --> 00:21:18,066
My son writes eight hours a day.
And he loves poetry.

446
00:21:18,133 --> 00:21:20,366
-Really?
-Which... I don’t understand poetry.

447
00:21:20,433 --> 00:21:22,933
-I don’t understand a single word.
-Poetry is bad standup.

448
00:21:24,066 --> 00:21:27,833
[Jerry] It’s carefully chosen words
that have no laugh at the end.

449
00:21:28,200 --> 00:21:29,466
[laughing]

450
00:21:36,395 --> 00:21:37,863
[Jerry] Another cool thing about this car.

451
00:21:37,933 --> 00:21:41,466
It has 99,900-something miles on it,

452
00:21:41,533 --> 00:21:42,399
and today,

453
00:21:42,466 --> 00:21:45,700
we will shoot this car
turning over the odometer.

454
00:21:45,766 --> 00:21:50,100
It’s about to go over
100,000 miles. Look at this.

455
00:21:50,166 --> 00:21:51,500
[engine revving]

456
00:21:52,366 --> 00:21:55,533
Six. Seven.

457
00:21:56,399 --> 00:22:00,133
Eight. Nine.

458
00:22:02,220 --> 00:22:05,090
[Jerry groans] I can't do it.

459
00:22:10,729 --> 00:22:12,197
I just can’t do it.

